 ## Background
-The concept of adaptive density control in 3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced by Kerbl et al. in the original 3DGS paper [Parent]. The method uses accumulated view-space position gradients to identify regions requiring higher geometric detail. Over time, various extensions have been proposed to address specific limitations such as memory usage [S5], occlusion handling [S6, unofficial], dynamic scene modeling [6351-groupflow.md], and probabilistic sampling [7852-3dgs-mcmc.md]. The family has become a standard component of 3DGS training pipelines, with numerous variants focusing on efficiency, stability, and specific application domains like SLAM [S7, unofficial] and compression [S9].
+The concept of adaptive density control was introduced in the original 3D Gaussian Splatting paper by Kerbl et al. (2023) [S7, unofficial]. The method was designed to address the limitations of static point cloud initializations, which often fail to capture fine details or result in excessive memory usage due to over-sampling in simple regions. The original algorithm used view-space position gradients to trigger cloning (for small, high-gradient Gaussians) and splitting (for large, high-gradient Gaussians) every 100 iterations, alongside opacity-based pruning. Subsequent research has expanded this family significantly, introducing probabilistic approaches like MCMC [S4], multi-view consistency checks [S2], and dynamic deformation handling [S1]. The strategy is now a core component of nearly all 3DGS-based reconstruction and generation pipelines, including SLAM [S7, unofficial] and text-to-3D methods [S1].
 
